# mps3-nanosoc-platform / fpga/Makefile
#
# THREE LINES, AND THEY SHOULD STAY THREE LINES. Everything about THIS design
# belongs in design.mk; everything about the FLOW belongs in the toolkit.
#
# If you find yourself wanting to add a target here, it is almost certainly one
# of three things that already have a home:
#
#   a project check that must run with a stage   ->  a prerequisite-append rule
#                                                    at the BOTTOM of design.mk
#   project Tcl at a named point in a stage      ->  fpga/hooks/<seam>.tcl
#   a wholesale replacement of one flow step     ->  fpga/overrides/<step>.tcl
#
#     make help     the flow, in the order you run it
#     make check    is this project's contract complete? (no tool, under a second)
#     make doctor   can this machine run the tools?
#     make env      engine / this run / project contract, three blocks
#
# WHY design.mk IS INCLUDED BY NAME AND NOT DISCOVERED. There is exactly one
# manifest and it is the first thing anything reads. A glob here would let a
# second one appear (design.mk.bak, design-old.mk) and change the build
# depending on sort order.
#
# WHY THE ENGINE IS INCLUDED TWICE, AND WHY THAT IS FINE. design.mk ends with
# the same `include $(FPGA_FLOW_DIR)/mk/flow.mk`, so it is self-sufficient when
# some other makefile includes it directly. mk/flow.mk carries an include guard
# for exactly this, and it is load-bearing: without one, GNU make emits an
# "overriding recipe for target" WARNING per target - not an error - and then
# silently runs the SECOND definition of each. A build that works by warning is
# a build nobody reads the warnings of.
#
# Generated by fpga-flow-init on 2026-09-23.
#
# Copyright (C) 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

FPGA_DIR := $(CURDIR)

include $(FPGA_DIR)/design.mk
include $(FPGA_FLOW_DIR)/mk/flow.mk
